// briefingRisk scores how likely the briefed context is to break, on 0–1.
//
// Risk and confidence are different things and conflating them was a bug: the
// Learn handler stamps confidence = 1.0 on every success/partial lesson (it
// means "this knowledge is verified and fresh"), so reading confidence as risk
// turned every SOLVED task into a 🛑 high-risk alarm — and then showed that
// lesson's what_failed (the problem it had already fixed) as the warning text.
//
// Risk therefore comes from the outcome (failure = hazard, success =
// known-good pattern), scaled by how much we still trust the lesson. With the
// default 0.6 threshold a success lesson (0.30 × 1.0) drops out on its own,
// while a fresh failure lesson (0.90 × 1.0) still surfaces.
func briefingRisk(outcome string, confidence float64) float64 {
	if confidence <= 0 || confidence > 1 {
		confidence = 1.0 // absent/garbage confidence: judge on the outcome alone
	}
	return outcomeConfidence(outcome) * confidence
}

